sections.body:
The apostrophe is not the bug. String concatenation is the bug: the query is assembled by gluing user text into SQL, so the database parses whatever the user typed as instructions rather than as a value.

```php
// concatenation: the input is now SQL
$sql = "SELECT id, name FROM orders WHERE name = '" . $_GET['q'] . "'";

// parameterized: the input stays data, and can never become syntax
$stmt = $pdo->prepare('SELECT id, name FROM orders WHERE name = ?');
$stmt->execute([$_GET['q']]);
```

The fix is a prepared statement with a bound parameter, and it is the developer's change to make. What you contribute is the evidence that turns a flaky page into a defect: the URL with the parameter, the exact string the user typed, the timestamp in UTC, the status code, and the first line of the error body. Add the answer to one question the developer will ask: does the same request return different records for a different name.

Two rules keep you inside your role. Do not append `OR 1=1`, a `UNION`, or a quote-by-quote payload to prove the point on production: a request that returns other users' records is a data breach you caused, and the customer now has to be told. Any test that changes application behavior needs written authorization from the application owner, naming the host and the test window, and it belongs in the lab, not on the live portal.
